{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794391107,"nonce":"4YIFWHEece6DitkG2uoVqvK3ZHM=","title":"GPU animation P7: public API cleanup, standards additions and the semver plan","message":"Part of the GPU-deferral plan (umbrella issue). Runs alongside the phases; the breaking changes land together in 0.7.0.\n\nProject rules this must respect: CSS-first with web-standard semantics; visual change is declared, not Rust logic; motion is declared with `motion()` and CSS, with no per-call duration builders; the reactive vocabulary is Signal, Computed, Effect.\n\n**Where today's API conflicts with those rules**\n1. Per-call durations: `Motion::fade_in(ms)` and the rest of the `*_in/_out` shortcuts, `AnimationPreset::*(ms)` (about 30 constructors), `Motion::from_stylesheet(.., enter_ms, exit_ms)`, `AnimationBuilder::with_duration`, `VisualAnimationConfig::timed`. Deprecate in favour of CSS tokens and rules, then remove.\n2. Imperative animated values as the way to animate visuals: `AnimatedValue`, `Div::animate`, `Motion::translate_x(SharedAnimatedValue)` and the other binding setters. Keep them as the escape hatch for continuous app input (a drag, a gauge), document them as CPU-driven app state, and stop using them inside widgets (cn still uses `animate_bounds`, `bind_bool_targets` and a private `AnimatedValue` in the chart easer).\n3. Properties outside CSS: `rotate-x`, `rotate-y`, `translate-z`, `depth`, `light-*`, `surface*`, `glass-*`, `overflow-fade`. Standard equivalents exist for some (`transform: rotateX()`, `perspective`). Decide per property which are accepted domain extras.\n4. `State\u003cT\u003e` and `Derived` are still public next to Signal and Computed; `Reactive\u003cT\u003e::Bound(State\u003cT\u003e)` is built on them.\n\n**Additions (all web standards, all cheap once the GPU table exists)**: `steps()` and `linear()` timing functions; `animation-play-state`; individual `translate`, `scale`, `rotate` properties (the survey found `rotate` handled but no `translate:` or `scale:` in the property match); `animation-timeline` (scroll and view timelines); `will-change` as the promotion hint; `prefers-reduced-motion`; `view-transition-name`. Not supported today and worth listing: `animation-composition`, `transition-behavior`, `scroll-behavior`, `scroll-snap`, `overscroll-behavior`.\n\n**Removals**: `Div::animate_layout` and `LayoutAnimationConfig` (deprecated since 0.3.0, still in the prelude); the `motion_is_exiting` fields; `use_state_for*` family.\n\n**Semver.** One breaking release: `#[non_exhaustive]` on `RenderProps`, `KeyframeProperties`, `MotionBindings`, `ElementStyle` and the other animation-facing structs; the new `GpuPrimitive` field (construct through builders); the removals above. After that, new fields are additive.\n\n**Coordination with the sibling blinc_abi repo.** It matches `PropertyId` by literal id (adding variants is safe; renaming or removing Width, Padding, Gap, BorderWidth breaks it), uses `PartialPropertyUpdate`, `RenderProps`, `queue_prop_update_partial`, `take_pending_partial_prop_updates` and `binding::with_registry` directly, and reads blinc_gpu display lists. Its doc comment about `PropertyId` order is stale. Descriptors are plain data and are the natural ABI form for 36603e0; its host `set_visual` path and display-list decoder need the node-slot path.\n\n**Docs.** CHANGELOG and book page with each phase (project rule). `crates/blinc_animation/README.md` documents methods that do not exist; committed `README.md.tmp` files should go.\n\nAcceptance: each deprecation has a replacement shown in the book and CHANGELOG; the 0.7.0 breakage list is complete for this repo and blinc_abi.","files":null}]}